00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.600
How many people had a phone
that looked something like this?

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.560
Anybody old enough?

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.410
Yeah, we have plenty.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:09.590
So much engineering
energy went into trying

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:13.880
to figure out what to do with
that damn keypad and how to--

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:17.960
all kinds of clever
sliding things,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.230
and they didn't pay any
attention to the screen.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:24.320
And then somebody,
OK, probably Apple,

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.950
came up with a
great idea of what

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.720
if we didn't even have a keypad?

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.220
And I think those are kind of
the most exciting things where

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.700
it's like, all of a sudden
this constraint, the keyboard,

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.330
was gone.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.800
And the smartphones really
took off after that.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.380
So what's the big
constraint in databases?

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.774
Well, the big constraint
databases is the table scan.

00:01:45.774 --> 00:01:47.440
And you don't have
to take that from me.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.450
You can take that from Oracle,
who's invested more engineering

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.420
energy in building vast
databases than probably

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:54.200
anybody.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.920
And they say basically
table scans are a nightmare.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.130
So what if you can we can
remove that constraint?

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:04.350
What if we can just
make table scans fast?

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.010
And so I'm going to start into
a little bit of how databases

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.440
work to sort of
build up into how

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.110
we can make table scans fast.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:15.560
So the primary data structure
for databases is a b-tree

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:16.690
or a b+ tree.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.520
And it effectively works by
building a tree of your data.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.310
So this select
query that's doing

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:26.990
a filter between these two
boundaries filter on this key,

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.657
we're able to fetch that
data doing only five

00:02:30.657 --> 00:02:32.990
look ups because we only have
to read these five blocks.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.670
And I say chances
are the top part

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.086
of the tree is
going to be cached,

00:02:36.086 --> 00:02:38.330
so it might only be two blocks.

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.970
Problem is, what if
you want to run a query

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:42.560
that you don't have--

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.960
you can't filter
conveniently on the key?

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.940
Well, then you have to
read the whole thing.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.250
And that takes a long time.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.730
So one way that people have
tried to scale databases

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:57.830
is they scale it out by a base
basically taking a database

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.990
and running it sort
of in multiple nodes

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:02.750
and then with some lightweight
coordination on top.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:04.520
I won't mention any
competitors, but this

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.459
is what a lot of people do.

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:08.250
And there's a couple
of problems with this.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:12.500
One is that you're limited
to the storage that's

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:13.590
attached to that node.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.999
So it's hard to scale that up.

00:03:15.999 --> 00:03:17.790
And the other one is,
I mean, first of all,

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:22.700
also like the coordination
effort gets really difficult.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.950
In another session
concurrently where

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:29.870
people are talking about
BigQuery geospatial advances

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:33.350
that we're launching today, and
one of the things that it does

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:36.680
is it does-- you can do a
cross join that says show me

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.870
the points from this table
that are near the points

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:40.250
from this table.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:44.330
In an architecture like
this, that's super difficult

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:48.270
because you have to send all of
this data between these nodes.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.530
So the network bandwidth
becomes a big constraint.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.740
So what did the
folks at Google do?

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.880
The Dremel paper came out 2010.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.590
Dremel had been around
for several years,

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:02.630
several years before then.

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:07.040
But effectively, the way they
approached scaling was we're

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.990
just going to scale
everything out

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:11.600
super wide so each one
of these sort of boxes

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.090
is a separate machine
now or is a separate task

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.190
or a separate container
running in Bourg.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.190
And we're going to make
everything a table scan,

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:24.350
because we have our fast
distributed file system, which

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:25.345
is which is Colossus.

00:04:25.345 --> 00:04:27.470
So we can read from hundreds
of thousands of disks.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.480
We can read on 100,000 cores.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:33.180
And we can make this
table scan fast.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.020
So we've been able to remove
a bunch of database design

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:37.200
constraints.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.490
There's the I/O constraints.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:40.430
Disks are slow.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.280
If you read from a
small number of disks,

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:44.870
that's going to be painful.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:49.280
Attached storage, you can only
deal with the storage that's

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.790
on that machine.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.490
CPUs aren't getting faster.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:56.635
Moore's law has sort of
ended for CPU speeds.

00:04:56.635 --> 00:04:58.010
And there's also,
as I mentioned,

00:04:58.010 --> 00:04:59.646
the network
coordination bottleneck.

00:04:59.646 --> 00:05:00.770
So how does BigQuery do it?

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:03.680
BigQuery/Dremel, because
Dremel is the query engine

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:04.824
that powers BigQuery.

00:05:04.824 --> 00:05:06.990
Well, it's going to read
from lots and lots of disks

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:07.624
in parallel.

00:05:07.624 --> 00:05:09.540
We have the separation
of storage and compute,

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.067
so you don't have any
scaling bottlenecks.

00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:14.400
As you know, I said Lloyd and
I were chatting yesterday.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.620
He says, you can't fill it up.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:18.964
He says that, not me.

00:05:18.964 --> 00:05:20.380
I know how many
disks we have, so.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.272
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:22.272 --> 00:05:25.350
And we can use thousands
of CPUs in parallel.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:26.600
And we can use our--

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.820
Google's very fast
Jupiter infrastructure.

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:33.630
So the Dremel architecture
when we released it sort of

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.280
looks similar to that tree.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.460
This is a diagram from
the original Dremel paper.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:42.180
One of the things
we soon learned

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.930
when we released BigQuery
is the log scanning

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:47.690
is not data warehousing.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:50.730
People, if they're going
to remove their analysis

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:53.950
onto BigQuery, we have to
be good at data warehousing.

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:58.120
So we made a big bet on
dynamic query planning

00:05:58.120 --> 00:05:58.980
and optimization.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.160
And what this means
is we will dynamically

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.470
change the query
plan depending on how

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:08.220
that query runs so that we can
run it in a optimal distributed

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:08.820
fashion.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:12.750
So 2018, this is sort of
a stylized version of what

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.250
the current architecture is.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.040
It's no longer a fixed tree.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.270
Basically every--
there's number of stages.

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:22.410
Each stage goes through
a shuffle, which

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.230
is a fast in-memory shuffle.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.430
And finally, you have
the reduce at the end.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:30.180
And we can dynamically change
how many shards we're using.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.060
We can dynamically change the--

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:36.480
whether we want to do a
broadcast or a hash join.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.360
We can dynamically change how
many stages, in fact, we're

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.460
running.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.540
So BigQuery as a data warehouse.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.810
I'm going to try to convince
you that BigQuery has,

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:50.454
and I think a lot of
other talks at Next

00:06:50.454 --> 00:06:52.620
have also talked about
BigQuery as a data warehouse.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.440
And I'm going to go
through some of the myths

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:00.180
and how we've overcome them,
or how those are not actually

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:00.940
true.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.690
So one of the myths is, and
I think that this is even

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.580
in some of our
documentation, that

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.520
says you should
denormalize your data when

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:09.450
you're using BigQuery.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.390
And if you're in an enterprise
data warehousing situation,

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:14.034
and you want to
move your Teradata

00:07:14.034 --> 00:07:16.200
or you want to move whatever
your existing system is

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.230
into BigQuery you're like well I
don't want to change my schema.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:22.120
I just want my old
queries to run.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.754
So we understand that.

00:07:23.754 --> 00:07:25.920
And so one of the things
that we've been working on,

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:29.460
because for enterprise
data warehousing

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:31.480
is you need joints to work fast.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.260
And so this is sort of
how we've done over the--

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:39.830
this is the TPCH benchmark with
10 terabytes over the last 18

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:40.330
months.

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:43.150
And so we've increased
performance in the geomean.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:47.790
So this isn't-- we're not cherry
picking individual queries

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:48.700
to run here.

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:53.820
But we've gotten 5x
faster across the board.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:08:00.540
This is TPCDS, which is
another common data warehousing

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:01.380
benchmark.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:02.970
That has also increased by 5x.

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.790
And I'm just going to show
you that last little bit

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.280
at the end, that
little dive down

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:13.380
is a performance improvement
that we just sort of rolled out

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:13.910
this week.

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:15.660
So can we switch over
to the demo, please?

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:22.430
So this is the TPCDS benchmark.

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:26.440
And this is sort of using
kind of the old system.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.100
And I had this run.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:32.679
And we can see that this ran,
it ran over 675 gigabytes.

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:35.590
And it took a minute
and 10 seconds.

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:38.539
I'm not going to make you wait
for a minute and 10 seconds

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:39.860
while that runs.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:41.679
But I will switch over
to the new version.

00:08:41.679 --> 00:08:48.970
The new version is exactly the
same query, that TPCDS query 6.

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:51.580
And we can run this,
we can run this now.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:54.320
And it's run over-- the
query is exactly the same.

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:57.124
The only difference
is it's running

00:08:57.124 --> 00:08:59.290
against sort of the clustered
version of the tables.

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:03.730
And I'll describe clustering
in a couple of minutes.

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:04.550
There we go.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:07.490
So 13.3 seconds versus--

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.871
so this is a 5x improvement.

00:09:09.871 --> 00:09:11.620
And this is just sort
of the kind of thing

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:16.380
that we roll out on a
Thursday, because it's ready.

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:17.714
Can we switch back, please?

00:09:17.714 --> 00:09:20.618
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:25.380
Can we switch back to
the slides, please?

00:09:28.537 --> 00:09:29.890
There.

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:34.560
OK, so now sort of we're
advertising our best practices.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.030
Don't denormalize if
you don't need to.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:41.620
If your schema is more natural
in the denormalized state,

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:44.230
then by all means
denormalize it,

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:46.900
or if the queries are
simpler, or if you're doing

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:51.130
joins against a large
dimension table.

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:52.630
So let's say you
have a star schema,

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:54.790
and you have your
fact table, and then

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:58.010
your other table
is fairly large,

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:01.520
you might consider
denormalizing.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:03.320
But that's also something
we're working on.

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:04.270
And we're working on--

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:05.853
the reason I have
an asterisk by large

00:10:05.853 --> 00:10:07.740
is because actually
what large means

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.066
should be increasing over time.

00:10:10.066 --> 00:10:12.190
And as I mentioned, joins
have been getting faster.

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:13.700
And they will continue
to get faster.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:15.449
We've got a bunch of
things on our roadmap

00:10:15.449 --> 00:10:20.120
that hopefully we're going to
continue to improve things.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:23.200
So I was chatting with
Lloyd, my co-presenter

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.622
here, a couple of months ago.

00:10:24.622 --> 00:10:26.080
And he said to me,
I love BigQuery.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.480
But you guys need to
improve partitioning.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:30.460
And we're like
yeah, yeah, we know.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:32.937
He actually didn't use as--

00:10:32.937 --> 00:10:34.270
didn't say it as nicely as that.

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:39.770
But this is-- he approved
this version of the slide.

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:41.715
So the version of
partitioning that we've had,

00:10:41.715 --> 00:10:43.090
and we've had for
a long time, is

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:44.460
ingestion-based partitioning.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.570
And I kind of just
want to go through how

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:50.610
this works so that I can show
what we're doing differently.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.330
Excuse me.

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:56.350
So BigQuery is a column
store, or has a column store.

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:59.350
So when you run
a select, we only

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:03.490
have to read the-- we only
charge you for the columns

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:04.450
that you read.

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:08.790
So in old BigQuery, we kind
of read down the columns,

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.450
but partitioning actually
lets us do a slice across.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:15.280
So if you have a
partition filter,

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:18.400
we're using this sort of
magical pseudo column that's

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:20.610
based on ingestion time.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:24.610
But that does let you
select only those bits,

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:28.090
and only have to pay for
reading the data that's

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:30.600
in that sort of intersection.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.410
And only have to-- so
it's not just cost,

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:38.120
but it's also
performance improvement.

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:41.020
So just recently, we made
column-based partitioning

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:41.962
generally available.

00:11:41.962 --> 00:11:43.420
Column-based
partitioning means you

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:46.060
don't have to use the
special pseudo column.

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:50.411
This means that you can
use a column in your table,

00:11:50.411 --> 00:11:51.910
and that's your
partitioning column.

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:56.425
And queries on that will just
sort of naturally filter.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:02.956
That's great.

00:12:02.956 --> 00:12:04.580
That's been in the
process for a while.

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:08.790
That's not really going to move
the needle for the most part.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:10.670
But today we're
announcing clustering.

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:14.180
And clustering, I think,
is really pretty cool.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.610
And that takes
partitioning sort of way

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:20.160
beyond how it had worked before.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.440
I'm going to just--

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:25.250
when you have table cluster,
you have your partition key,

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:27.200
but then you also have
your clustering key.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:28.700
And what a lot of
people like to use

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:31.820
is like a user ID
or a customer ID.

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:35.380
And BigQuery will actually
store this in sorted format.

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:38.700
S-O-R-T-E-D, not S-O-R-D-I-D.

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:41.090
So when you filter
based on that,

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:42.920
you only have to read the--

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:45.466
we can read a very,
very small amount.

00:12:45.466 --> 00:12:47.840
So to give you just sort of
an example of how this works,

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:50.000
so first we apply
the partition filter.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.430
Then we apply the
cluster filter.

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:54.650
And we only have to read
a very, very small sliver

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:55.850
of that table.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.020
So queries are going
to be not only faster,

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:01.470
but they're going to
be less expensive.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:04.100
So clustering works with a
high cardinal-- generally

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:06.470
you use it when you have
a high cardinality field.

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:11.060
You only pay for scanning blocks
that match your cluster key.

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:12.057
Its going to be faster.

00:13:12.057 --> 00:13:13.640
And it's going to
be easier to manage,

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:15.140
because we have a
lot of people that

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:18.830
are kind of manually sharding
one table per customer.

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:21.800
Now you can put that in one
table and just do a filter.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:25.205
And it's sort of more
natural data management.

00:13:25.205 --> 00:13:26.330
Switch to the demo, please.

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:34.990
So I'm just going to start this.

00:13:34.990 --> 00:13:37.170
So last year, maybe
it was two years ago,

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:43.630
at Cloud Next I ran this query
over this giant petabyte table

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:44.590
that we had.

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:52.600
This petabyte table is a
simulated retail schema.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:56.590
And we can see kind of
what is in this table.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:14:01.060
It's got sales, and then you
can join out to products.

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:04.060
And so I'm doing a select
star, which you rarely

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:07.000
do in BigQuery because
it reads all the columns.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:11.030
And this is doing
a filter by key.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:12.430
So this is the clustering key.

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:15.190
Now one of the things that
I didn't show last time

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:17.800
was I didn't show how
much this query costs.

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:21.130
Because when I ran
it two years ago,

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:23.353
this was actually
a $5,000 query.

00:14:23.353 --> 00:14:25.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:29.220
BigQuery is fast and it
scales, but you pay for that.

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:34.060
So we say, well, you can scale
it, can scale it to this.

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:36.940
And many people who
have this size table

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:40.030
would be using
fixed-price reservations,

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:44.620
so they wouldn't have
to pay per query.

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:47.090
But if you're going to
pay sort of retail prices,

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:48.340
that's how much it would cost.

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:52.840
And when I ran this, when
I ran this two years ago,

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:55.620
it took 270 seconds.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:56.500
So I think it was--

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:59.300
I think it was
almost five minutes.

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:02.620
So we made some performance
improvement since then.

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:06.612
And usually this takes, well,
we'll see how long this takes.

00:15:06.612 --> 00:15:08.320
It's going to take a
couple more seconds.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:12.770
I'm going to switch to next
one and come back to it.

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:15.900
So that was just a
scan filter query.

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:17.980
And as I promised
you, BigQuery is not

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:20.110
just good at log scanning.

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:23.620
BigQuery is also
good at doing joins.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:28.390
And so actually last year one
of the things that I did was I

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:30.742
showed how to do
query optimization.

00:15:30.742 --> 00:15:32.200
And one of the
things that I showed

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.170
was an optimization was
OK, if you're joining--

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:39.150
if your filter is on the
wrong side of the join key,

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:41.500
or the join constraint,
then your query

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:43.940
is going to be really-- your
query can be really slow.

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:47.500
So this is just showing that
a, not only have we fixed that

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:52.330
by being able to push the
constraints through the query,

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:54.940
but also now we
can do-- let's see,

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:59.470
this was-- this query over the
petabyte table, 3.5 seconds.

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:05.000
We're actually doing a real
join against a real table.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:09.532
All right now let's jump back
to this query, which finished in

00:16:09.532 --> 00:16:10.490
just under two minutes.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:14.660
So we're about twice as fast
as we were two years ago.

00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:16.550
Things get faster.

00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:18.050
But one of the cool
things is if you

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:22.860
look at how much data we
process, it's half a gigabyte.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:24.200
So this is what you pay for.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:27.710
So we've gone from
$5,000 to this

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:30.330
is about a tenth of a penny.

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:32.330
So this is, what, like
four orders of magnitude,

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:36.787
five orders of magnitude
price improvement.

00:16:36.787 --> 00:16:39.769
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:44.709 --> 00:16:46.000
And back to the slides, please.

00:16:49.177 --> 00:16:51.510
So in case you're wondering
when do you use partitioning

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:52.950
or when do you use clustering?

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:54.594
So they are different.

00:16:54.594 --> 00:16:56.010
If you have a
database background,

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.600
people tend to be
familiar with these terms.

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:01.920
If not, it may be a
little bit confusing.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:04.500
Partitioning is generally
when you have a small number

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:06.450
of distinct values.

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:10.740
Less than 10,000 is our maximum
for partitioning right now.

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:12.630
Clustering can be
any cardinality.

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:16.890
You can have millions or
billions of different values.

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:21.240
In BigQuery, the dry run pricing
for partitioning is exact.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:22.859
So before you run
the query, we'll

00:17:22.859 --> 00:17:24.510
tell you how much it cost--

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:25.710
how much it's going to cost.

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:28.830
For cluster tables,
we're going to tell you

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:31.140
the maximum it could cost.

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:36.140
And if you have a clustering
filter, it could be a lot less.

00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:38.410
That's sort of what you give up.

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:41.847
And so the query pricing
is exact for partitioning.

00:17:41.847 --> 00:17:43.180
It's best effort for clustering.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:45.240
So if you're writing
a lot to your table,

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:47.014
we may not have
sorted it completely.

00:17:47.014 --> 00:17:48.930
And so we're going to
charge based on whatever

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:52.000
we have to end up reading.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:54.210
There is a small
performance overhead

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:59.720
for clustered tables per
partition that you're scanning.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.050
And clustering doesn't
suffer from any of that.

00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:04.290
But on the other hand,
partitioning can be--

00:18:04.290 --> 00:18:06.660
you can do data management
like it's a table.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:10.550
You can have expiration times on
your clusters whereas-- sorry,

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:15.330
on your partitions, where on
clustering you have to use DML.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:18.632
OK so I want to address some
other myths about BigQuery.

00:18:18.632 --> 00:18:20.340
So one thing people
say is well, BigQuery

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:22.710
can't do row and
column level security.

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.100
So we do it in a
slightly different way.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:26.512
You can use authorized views.

00:18:26.512 --> 00:18:28.470
And so this has been
available for a long time,

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:30.261
but I kind of want to
describe it because I

00:18:30.261 --> 00:18:31.900
think it's under utilized.

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:34.080
So first up, you can
create a view of the data

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:35.224
that you want to share.

00:18:35.224 --> 00:18:37.140
And that could filter
out the rows and columns

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:40.680
you don't want to use, or you
don't want somebody to see.

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:45.670
And then you add that view to
the ACL of the source dataset.

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:47.970
Now what this does is
it lets the person who

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:50.640
owns that dataset see
exactly who has access.

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:52.864
So we're not doing kind
of delegated authorization

00:18:52.864 --> 00:18:54.780
or anything, and you
don't have to worry about

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:57.240
if the person who created
that then leaves the company.

00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:57.840
You can audit.

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:01.590
You can actually see
what's on that ACL.

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:04.730
So just to show kind of how
this works on a typical table,

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:07.705
let's say you have a
phone number which has PII

00:19:07.705 --> 00:19:10.150
and you don't want
people to see it.

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:12.220
And maybe you have some
sort of like special rows

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:15.430
that you also want
to keep private.

00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:17.590
So you just create
a view definition

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:21.310
that, let's say, it filters out
the rows you don't want to show

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:26.400
and hashes the phone number.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:29.770
And so the person--
so by default

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:34.870
you can only see
this view if you

00:19:34.870 --> 00:19:36.770
have access to underlying data.

00:19:36.770 --> 00:19:40.330
So the next thing you do is
you can add to the data set.

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:43.390
You can say allow this
view access to this table.

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:47.490
And then you can share
access to that view.

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:49.740
So one of the things we like
to talk about in BigQuery

00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:51.490
is just how easy it
is to share your data.

00:19:51.490 --> 00:19:54.040
And we see this as a positive.

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:57.520
Lloyd will also describe
a little bit about that.

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:01.100
But I was talking to somebody
at a briefing in Texas,

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.992
and he's like, well, easy
data sharing, that sounds

00:20:03.992 --> 00:20:05.200
like a bug and not a feature.

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:06.741
That means that
somebody can share it

00:20:06.741 --> 00:20:09.190
with somebody that I don't
want it to be shared with.

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:15.160
So Google Virtual Private Cloud
is something that's in beta.

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.050
And that's going to
allow you to prevent--

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:18.790
and so we could have kind
of implemented something

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:20.950
like this just for BigQuery,
but this is actually

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:25.540
sort of been implemented across
Google's Cloud using kind

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:26.900
of standardized mechanisms.

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:28.510
So you'll be able
to prevent anybody

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:32.404
from sharing your BigQuery data
outside of your organization.

00:20:32.404 --> 00:20:34.570
You'll be able to define
which services can be used,

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:39.340
like so maybe you
can't export it to GCS.

00:20:39.340 --> 00:20:42.970
It allows you kind of to control
the routes of data filtration

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:45.670
and cut it off completely or
cut it off to certain people

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:47.650
or certain buckets.

00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:49.630
And you can also say
that this can only

00:20:49.630 --> 00:20:52.480
be accessed from your
organization's IP range.

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:55.270
And so with like
the virtual cluster,

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:58.840
you can have stuff in your
Compute Engine instances

00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:01.680
that look like they're from
your organization's IP range,

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:03.640
and so those will
just magically work,

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:10.280
whereas some random hacker is
not going to be able to access.

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:12.340
Another thing that
people tend to want

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:14.631
is they want to be able to
control their own encryption

00:21:14.631 --> 00:21:15.170
keys.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:17.340
So BigQuery has a generally
available feature,

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:21.700
we have since February,
that allows you to manage

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:23.470
your own encryption keys.

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:28.210
And so this sort of reduces
the kind of debug ability

00:21:28.210 --> 00:21:29.530
from the BigQuery side.

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:31.540
Like we can't go
in and figure out

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:34.480
what went wrong with
BigQuery as easily

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:40.240
without getting
explicit approval.

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:41.740
And also, it allows
you to basically

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:45.390
just be in control of how
encryption is being applied.

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:49.660
So this is sort of how
the encryption works.

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:52.630
And the final decryption
actually happens all the way

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:54.050
down to the storage layer.

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:56.980
And the key material
is actually--

00:21:56.980 --> 00:22:00.640
never flows through
the BigQuery servers.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:03.600
Nor does the encryption or
decryption happen there.

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:07.890
So I also want to share--

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:11.890
so with GDPR and with
other requirements,

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:15.820
people want to have
clean ways to manage

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:21.486
their personal identifiable
information their PII data.

00:22:21.486 --> 00:22:23.110
And so I want to
share a design pattern

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:26.880
that one of our customers
uses that uses encryption

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:33.130
to control who has access to
PII and actually remove it.

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:37.100
So for sort of if somebody
wants to be forgotten.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:38.770
So let's say you have
a table with PII.

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:40.630
This is sort of the basic table.

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:43.600
The phone number column is PII.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.380
So you can encrypt
that with a key.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:49.810
You can encrypt that with a
key per row or per user ID.

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:54.430
You have a separate table
that has the keys that

00:22:54.430 --> 00:22:56.480
match up with each user ID.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:00.380
So when you read that, you
can run an AES decryption,

00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:04.236
which is the new functions that
we have to decrypt the data.

00:23:04.236 --> 00:23:06.610
And the nice thing about this
is you can share this table

00:23:06.610 --> 00:23:09.670
with anybody you want.

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:10.870
They can copy that table.

00:23:10.870 --> 00:23:12.790
You don't have to
necessarily chase it down,

00:23:12.790 --> 00:23:14.680
but they can't actually
read the PII in it

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:17.590
unless they have access to
this underlying keys table.

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:22.840
And then if-- for the
right to be forgotten,

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:25.960
or you want to get
rid of that user,

00:23:25.960 --> 00:23:30.310
crypto delete is as
simple as just doing a DML

00:23:30.310 --> 00:23:32.934
delete from that keys table.

00:23:32.934 --> 00:23:35.350
So you don't have to go back
and figure out all the places

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:36.910
where that PII went.

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:41.530
It just can now no
longer be accessed.

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:46.570
Another myth is that people
are worried that BigQuery makes

00:23:46.570 --> 00:23:49.570
it easy to run up a big bill.

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:51.250
We now have self-service
cost controls.

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:52.150
I have one more
demo, if you don't

00:23:52.150 --> 00:23:53.460
mind switching over, please.

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:00.370
Thank you.

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:02.510
And so I just want to
show how this works.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:04.960
This is the Google
Cloud console.

00:24:04.960 --> 00:24:07.617
You can get here from--

00:24:07.617 --> 00:24:08.200
where'd it go?

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.540
I am an admin, and quotas.

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:17.710
Filter by BigQuery API, and
you can change the allowable--

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:20.780
if I can click on this, no.

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:27.754
You can change the
allowable usage per day.

00:24:27.754 --> 00:24:31.148
I can say edit quotas.

00:24:31.148 --> 00:24:32.725
Sorry, this is Lloyd's laptop.

00:24:32.725 --> 00:24:33.850
He doesn't have permission.

00:24:33.850 --> 00:24:35.480
But had Lloyd did--

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:37.980
which I guess is a good thing.

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:40.220
We don't need him to
change our quotas.

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:42.920
But had we had permission, we
could just say edit quotas here

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:46.730
for the BigQuery API,
the query uses per day.

00:24:46.730 --> 00:24:48.390
And that gets
applied immediately.

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:49.820
So that's across your project.

00:24:49.820 --> 00:24:52.580
There's also a per user
one that you can say.

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:54.980
So if you want to say
each user should only

00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:57.800
be able to process 10 terabytes
a day, you can set that.

00:24:57.800 --> 00:24:59.030
You can set that here.

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:00.750
That one doesn't get
applied immediately.

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:02.874
There's a little bit of a delay.

00:25:02.874 --> 00:25:04.790
But at least there's
sort of self-service ways

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:07.700
that you can manage that.

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:08.900
Back to the slides, please.

00:25:12.559 --> 00:25:14.100
Something else that
is sort of a myth

00:25:14.100 --> 00:25:16.830
is that BigQuery doesn't
allow enough DML updates.

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:18.410
We do have batched
DML operations.

00:25:18.410 --> 00:25:22.020
So often if people are used to
do sort of individual deletes,

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:23.760
you can delete
from an array, you

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.440
can delete based on another--

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:26.190
based on a filter.

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:29.539
You can delete based on a
join from another table.

00:25:29.539 --> 00:25:31.080
But there's another
really cool thing

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:34.324
that's in beta right now
is the SQL MERGE statement.

00:25:34.324 --> 00:25:36.240
And I wasn't aware of
the SQL MERGE statement.

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:40.070
It's actually a
pretty slick thing.

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:43.274
So I want to go through how
this works a little bit.

00:25:43.274 --> 00:25:44.940
So let's say we have
a customer's table,

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:47.730
and let's say we have another
table that has our updates.

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:49.560
And we want to sort
of apply those updates

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:52.027
to the customer's table.

00:25:52.027 --> 00:25:53.610
So the first thing
we do is we create.

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:55.770
We start our MERGE statement.

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:57.240
And we say we're going to merge.

00:25:57.240 --> 00:26:01.110
And we're going to kind of match
those up using the user ID.

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:06.970
And you can use complex
conditions here.

00:26:06.970 --> 00:26:08.610
So then we match those.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:10.780
You can then define
match conditions.

00:26:10.780 --> 00:26:13.710
So if there is a match between
the two, so between the user

00:26:13.710 --> 00:26:17.532
ID on one side and the
user ID on the other side,

00:26:17.532 --> 00:26:19.990
and you can even make those
conditions arbitrarily complex.

00:26:19.990 --> 00:26:23.370
You can say also if the table
on the left is not locked,

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:25.680
then we can do something,
and the thing we want to do

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:29.380
is update it to be the
thing that's on the right.

00:26:29.380 --> 00:26:32.890
So fancy animation.

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:36.330
We seen we've updated that row.

00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:38.229
But you can do more
with a single statement.

00:26:38.229 --> 00:26:40.020
You can also say, well,
when if there's not

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:41.850
a match, which means that
if there's not something

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:43.805
in the left table, or
if there's something

00:26:43.805 --> 00:26:46.740
in the right table that's
not in the left, in this case

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:48.780
we'll say well, we're
going to insert that

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:52.352
into the left-hand table.

00:26:52.352 --> 00:26:53.310
But wait, there's more.

00:26:53.310 --> 00:26:55.290
We can even say would not
match by source, which

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:56.915
means if something
is in the left table

00:26:56.915 --> 00:27:00.150
but not in the right table, we
can apply complex conditions

00:27:00.150 --> 00:27:01.500
as well.

00:27:01.500 --> 00:27:04.050
We can just say, in this case,
we're just saying delete.

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:07.150
But this just allows you to
do sort of really complex,

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:12.660
like read, insert,
update, deletes

00:27:12.660 --> 00:27:15.990
all in a single statement.

00:27:15.990 --> 00:27:18.660
All right, so I've
tried to convince you

00:27:18.660 --> 00:27:21.600
that BigQuery is a great
place to do data warehousing.

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:23.220
But I think one of
the things that I

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:24.750
showed with you,
the reason that I

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:28.890
had that, the initial removing
kind of removing constraints

00:27:28.890 --> 00:27:32.670
discussion, was I think
BigQuery can be more,

00:27:32.670 --> 00:27:35.330
is more than just
a data warehouse.

00:27:35.330 --> 00:27:37.924
And of course I'm biased.

00:27:37.924 --> 00:27:39.840
I've been working on
BigQuery for seven years.

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:43.020
And Google pays me to say
nice things about BigQuery.

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:47.250
So Lloyd's going to come up and
talk about sort of beyond just

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:50.220
normal data warehousing,
and the kinds of things

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:51.690
that he's been able to do.

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:55.170
So Lloyd is the
inventor of Looker.

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:58.500
He is a big kind of
BigQuery aficionado.

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:01.110
He's been able to push
BigQuery really further

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:03.739
than almost anybody
that I've seen.

00:28:03.739 --> 00:28:05.280
So I think there's
not anybody better

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:07.490
to show what else you
can do with BigQuery.

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:08.123
Thanks, Lloyd.

00:28:08.123 --> 00:28:09.247
LLOYD TABB: Thanks, Jordan.

00:28:09.247 --> 00:28:12.109
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:16.590
So as Jordan says,
I'm a bit of a geek.

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:19.050
So I'm not much of a speaker,
so I apologize in advance.

00:28:21.992 --> 00:28:25.752
I'm going to frame what you
can do with BigQuery first.

00:28:25.752 --> 00:28:27.210
I've been trying
to think about how

00:28:27.210 --> 00:28:29.876
to explain this for a long time,
since actually starting Looker.

00:28:29.876 --> 00:28:31.570
And I still never got it.

00:28:31.570 --> 00:28:33.850
It's never been perfect.

00:28:33.850 --> 00:28:35.700
So this is a new
way of describing it

00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:37.075
that I'm going to
try on you guys

00:28:37.075 --> 00:28:38.820
and see if it makes any sense.

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:43.380
In the traditional
reporting world,

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:48.250
I think of that world as
2D data and 2D information.

00:28:48.250 --> 00:28:52.480
And those-- that answers
questions about what happened.

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:55.410
So a traditional 2D thing might
be something like the New York

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:56.550
Times.

00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:59.310
The New York Times
is vetted every day.

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:01.530
It's a digest of the best--
of the most of the things

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:03.000
that happened that day.

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:04.920
You know that the
information is accurate.

00:29:04.920 --> 00:29:09.650
You can bank on the
veracity of the information.

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:10.630
And it's important.

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:14.130
A textbook is another
example of a 2D report.

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:15.930
And for the most
part in enterprise,

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:18.060
2D reporting is what you
run your business on.

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:19.870
The SEC is very care--

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:22.710
if you build a report and
it goes through the SEC,

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:25.932
you very much care about
the accuracy of that.

00:29:25.932 --> 00:29:27.390
There's another
kind of information

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:30.390
though that's happened since
the dawn of the internet.

00:29:30.390 --> 00:29:34.690
And I was lucky enough to
be around at the beginning.

00:29:34.690 --> 00:29:37.680
And that experience,
that 3D information

00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:40.620
is something like, say, Google,
where you can go ask a question

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:42.600
and do some research
about a subject.

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:45.090
So the 2D information, say--

00:29:45.090 --> 00:29:45.840
here's an example.

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:47.548
Suppose you're reading
The New York Times

00:29:47.548 --> 00:29:49.590
and you see a new competitor.

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.100
And you read about the company.

00:29:51.100 --> 00:29:54.390
Well, if you're interested in
that, you know that it exists,

00:29:54.390 --> 00:29:56.930
but there's not a lot more
that you can do with it,

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:58.710
with that piece of information.

00:29:58.710 --> 00:30:02.575
And pre-internet you you'd
run down to the library

00:30:02.575 --> 00:30:04.200
and look for other
periodicals about it

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:05.670
and see, or call people
and see if you could

00:30:05.670 --> 00:30:07.150
find some information about it.

00:30:07.150 --> 00:30:10.650
But in the post-internet world,
you go to Google and you type

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:13.452
in the company name, and
you look at LinkedIn,

00:30:13.452 --> 00:30:15.160
and you see how many
employees they have.

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:16.710
And you might look
on Crunch Base

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:19.339
and see who the
investors are, and who

00:30:19.339 --> 00:30:20.380
are the principals in it.

00:30:20.380 --> 00:30:22.800
And you might look on
Glassdoor to see whether or not

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:26.430
the employees like working
there and what's going.

00:30:26.430 --> 00:30:29.340
So there are other
ways of doing research

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:32.400
about something to
get a better picture,

00:30:32.400 --> 00:30:33.420
to get more perspective.

00:30:33.420 --> 00:30:36.127
So 2D is flat and
accurate and perfect.

00:30:36.127 --> 00:30:37.710
And super important,
because you can't

00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:39.266
run your business without that.

00:30:39.266 --> 00:30:40.890
And most of the time,
we think of data,

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:42.242
we think of it that way.

00:30:42.242 --> 00:30:44.200
But there's another way
of thinking about data,

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:45.810
which is the investigative way.

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:49.620
And it really hasn't really--

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:52.560
these new tools like BigQuery
allow that to happen.

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:54.930
So what's important
in that is that you

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:58.030
have a pretty comprehensive
source of information,

00:30:58.030 --> 00:30:58.920
so like the web.

00:30:58.920 --> 00:31:02.614
There's lots and lots of ways
to look at it, and to dig at it.

00:31:02.614 --> 00:31:04.780
And because of things like
these new data warehouses

00:31:04.780 --> 00:31:07.080
like BigQuery, you can put
all the data in one place

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:09.710
and then do BigQuery things,
like BigQuery and Looker can

00:31:09.710 --> 00:31:12.210
actually do the research to
find out information about that.

00:31:12.210 --> 00:31:16.200
And so the 2D information
answer is what happened.

00:31:16.200 --> 00:31:20.160
And the 3D is about diagnostics
about why it happened, right?

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:21.930
And there have been--
you can extract data

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:24.896
in with small data sets, try to
look at why something happened.

00:31:24.896 --> 00:31:27.020
So people have been interested
in that for a while.

00:31:27.020 --> 00:31:28.561
But really to get
a holistic picture,

00:31:28.561 --> 00:31:30.660
you need the whole set.

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:33.870
So that's what I think that you
should be thinking about trying

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:35.800
to build out of BigQuery.

00:31:35.800 --> 00:31:42.210
And so I'm going to give you an
example of 3D data experience.

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:45.180
One of our early customers
was a hotel aggregator.

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:46.950
And there was a
marketing person.

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:50.640
And they had a 2D report,
which was they were

00:31:50.640 --> 00:31:51.765
looking at their user base.

00:31:51.765 --> 00:31:53.640
And they were trying to
figure out referrals,

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:55.530
because cost of acquisition
for this business

00:31:55.530 --> 00:31:57.150
was super important, right?

00:31:57.150 --> 00:31:58.200
In order to be able
to drive business,

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:00.283
they got to drive the cost
of customer acquisition

00:32:00.283 --> 00:32:02.200
down so that they could then--

00:32:02.200 --> 00:32:04.960
and to spend more on
the platform over time.

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:06.199
So they had a little report.

00:32:06.199 --> 00:32:06.990
It was just a grid.

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:11.407
And on the one corner,
it was how many bookings

00:32:11.407 --> 00:32:13.240
they had going across
and how many referrals

00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:14.380
they had going down.

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:19.530
And in the lower left-hand
corner, there was a number.

00:32:19.530 --> 00:32:21.560
And it was big.

00:32:21.560 --> 00:32:23.310
So these were people
who had never ordered

00:32:23.310 --> 00:32:24.379
but referred a lot.

00:32:24.379 --> 00:32:25.920
They had people who
had ordered a lot

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:27.490
and referred a
lot, that was that.

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:29.100
But what surprised
them was the number

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:31.386
of people who had ordered--

00:32:31.386 --> 00:32:32.760
who'd never ordered
but referred.

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:34.030
And they were like,
were we getting gamed?

00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:34.660
What's going on?

00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:36.118
In a 2D world,
you're kind of done.

00:32:36.118 --> 00:32:37.320
That's it.

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:39.200
I got to go back to the
data team to get it.

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:40.575
But in a 3D world,
they can click

00:32:40.575 --> 00:32:42.110
on it, which isolates the data.

00:32:42.110 --> 00:32:45.990
Basically would isolate it to
the both of those constraints,

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:47.880
never ordered and
referred a lot.

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:49.740
And they could then
look at the detail.

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:52.107
So it's isolation and
drilling into the detail.

00:32:52.107 --> 00:32:53.940
And then they got a
list of email addresses.

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:56.444
And they said well, they
weren't an engineer like me,

00:32:56.444 --> 00:32:58.110
so they were marketers,
so they were not

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:00.443
afraid of actually emailing
them and saying, who are you

00:33:00.443 --> 00:33:01.775
and why are you doing this?

00:33:01.775 --> 00:33:04.200
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:05.400
And they got it.

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:07.020
And it came back
I'm a taxi driver,

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:09.420
I'm an airline stewardess,
I'm a bartender,

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.050
I'm someone who gets
asked the question

00:33:11.050 --> 00:33:13.510
where should I stay
tonight, right?

00:33:13.510 --> 00:33:16.040
And that changed
their business, right?

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:19.810
That ability to actually walk
around and drill into the data,

00:33:19.810 --> 00:33:23.050
actually going from
the 2D perspective

00:33:23.050 --> 00:33:28.270
through the 3D perspective gave
them a chance to see more data,

00:33:28.270 --> 00:33:32.530
to understand why
that was happening.

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:34.700
I'll give you another example.

00:33:34.700 --> 00:33:38.485
So Looker, I've been working
on Looker for six years.

00:33:38.485 --> 00:33:41.110
But I've been working, I've been
a CTO at a bunch of companies.

00:33:41.110 --> 00:33:43.948
And one of the
companies I was at,

00:33:43.948 --> 00:33:46.570
I was the CTO of an
online crowdsourced call

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:48.310
center with 30,000
home-based operators.

00:33:48.310 --> 00:33:51.205
And my job was to make it so
that everybody in the company

00:33:51.205 --> 00:33:51.830
could see data.

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:53.530
So I built some
special purpose tools.

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:56.680
And we have this measure
that we use called a chup.

00:33:56.680 --> 00:33:59.870
Who knows what a chup is?

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:03.190
A chup is a phone call where
the caller hung up before it

00:34:03.190 --> 00:34:05.120
came to an operator, right?

00:34:05.120 --> 00:34:09.428
And so how you--
so an operational

00:34:09.428 --> 00:34:10.969
measure that we
picked was the number

00:34:10.969 --> 00:34:13.880
of chups over the number
of total phone calls.

00:34:13.880 --> 00:34:16.400
What percentage of
calls were chups?

00:34:16.400 --> 00:34:18.110
And then we would look
at this over time.

00:34:18.110 --> 00:34:20.110
And in the middle of the
day, we saw a big spike

00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:22.620
that said there was a bunch
of chups in an hour period.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:23.510
So we isolated it.

00:34:23.510 --> 00:34:26.210
So we were like, well,
why are there chups?

00:34:26.210 --> 00:34:30.694
And so the 2D report
said there was a problem,

00:34:30.694 --> 00:34:32.110
but we wanted to
do some analysis.

00:34:32.110 --> 00:34:33.840
So the way that you
start to do analysis

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:35.280
is you isolate it to that.

00:34:35.280 --> 00:34:37.539
You filter on the
range that's there.

00:34:37.539 --> 00:34:39.830
And you start to dimensionalize
it by different things.

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:41.570
You can dimensionalize
it by operator.

00:34:41.570 --> 00:34:43.400
Well, was there one operator
who wasn't picking up?

00:34:43.400 --> 00:34:45.139
Was it we were routing to them
and they weren't picking up?

00:34:45.139 --> 00:34:46.670
No, that wasn't the problem.

00:34:46.670 --> 00:34:49.190
Was it one program that we had
a whole bunch of phone calls

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:51.630
for a particular program that
we were answering calls for?

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:53.039
No, that wasn't the problem.

00:34:53.039 --> 00:34:54.830
Well, let's isolate it
by telepathy switch.

00:34:54.830 --> 00:34:57.500
Oh look, this switch
is broken, right?

00:34:57.500 --> 00:35:00.122
And so by
dimensionalizing the data

00:35:00.122 --> 00:35:02.330
and walking around the data,
you can actually see it.

00:35:02.330 --> 00:35:04.010
And so these are
the kinds of things

00:35:04.010 --> 00:35:05.426
you can build out
of a modern data

00:35:05.426 --> 00:35:10.020
warehouse and a data platform
like Looker that's not possible

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:10.520
before.

00:35:13.040 --> 00:35:14.930
So traditional
data warehousing is

00:35:14.930 --> 00:35:18.380
all about the accurate
report, right?

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:20.210
It's all about getting
the data right.

00:35:20.210 --> 00:35:22.790
So in order to do
this, what you do

00:35:22.790 --> 00:35:24.840
is that you vet the
data on the way in.

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:29.750
So the traditional
way of putting data

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:31.510
into a data warehouse
is that it's small,

00:35:31.510 --> 00:35:32.426
it's relatively small.

00:35:32.426 --> 00:35:33.830
The data warehouse is expensive.

00:35:33.830 --> 00:35:34.830
Storage is expensive.

00:35:34.830 --> 00:35:38.240
So what you do is you take
your transactional systems,

00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:39.740
and you vet it, and
you compress it,

00:35:39.740 --> 00:35:41.114
and you put it
into the database,

00:35:41.114 --> 00:35:44.210
and you know that the
data is accurate once it

00:35:44.210 --> 00:35:46.220
lands in the warehouse, right?

00:35:46.220 --> 00:35:49.030
The whole idea is get
it in there, it's right.

00:35:49.030 --> 00:35:51.570
So the data team is
working as the ETL team.

00:35:51.570 --> 00:35:54.890
They're the person that makes
sure that it's there right.

00:35:54.890 --> 00:35:56.780
In the new data
warehousing model,

00:35:56.780 --> 00:35:58.730
you land the data
in the warehouse

00:35:58.730 --> 00:36:00.530
and then you build
models on top of that,

00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:03.980
and you code on top of
that to make-- so that you

00:36:03.980 --> 00:36:05.990
can draw more together
so that you have access

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:07.310
to all of the detail.

00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:12.050
And you build-- we say that you
build a model against that data

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:15.350
so that you know
that it's accurate.

00:36:15.350 --> 00:36:17.900
And when you pull it out,
when you pull the data out,

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:20.480
it comes through code as opposed
to coming through the way

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:21.398
that it went in.

00:36:24.150 --> 00:36:25.870
And so we've been
working together

00:36:25.870 --> 00:36:28.360
with BigQuery for
quite some time.

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:30.280
And together we make
this great platform

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:35.740
that you can use to do this
3D experience and data.

00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:39.200
They invited me up here because
I do wild things with BigQuery.

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:43.270
I'm going to show you a
couple of cool things.

00:36:43.270 --> 00:36:46.330
One thing that
BigQuery-- so I end up--

00:36:46.330 --> 00:36:48.520
Looker works against almost
every kind of database

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:49.020
there is.

00:36:49.020 --> 00:36:53.210
But I really love BigQuery's
SQL, because it is amazing.

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:55.120
It's amazing how
they've implemented it.

00:36:55.120 --> 00:36:58.630
And what's different about them
is their arrays, structures,

00:36:58.630 --> 00:37:00.460
the UDFs, and the
nesting, as well as

00:37:00.460 --> 00:37:05.260
the ability to handle just
astronomical amounts of data.

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:07.450
But the structures and
the UDFs and the nesting

00:37:07.450 --> 00:37:10.300
allow you to build stuff
that you can't possibly

00:37:10.300 --> 00:37:13.790
build in a traditional
data warehouse.

00:37:13.790 --> 00:37:16.940
And it all comes down to
a piece of technologies

00:37:16.940 --> 00:37:19.600
that arrays of
structs are really

00:37:19.600 --> 00:37:22.034
sub tables on a
single row of data.

00:37:22.034 --> 00:37:24.700
Has anybody here played with the
arrays and structs in BigQuery?

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:26.250
Oh, cool.

00:37:26.250 --> 00:37:28.230
And UDF?

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:30.670
Cool.

00:37:30.670 --> 00:37:31.834
It's super powerful.

00:37:31.834 --> 00:37:34.000
So actually, I'm going to
do a demo in a little bit.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:37.577
But everything comes
down to the fact that--

00:37:37.577 --> 00:37:38.410
I'll keep repeating.

00:37:38.410 --> 00:37:40.540
This slide is going to
appear a couple of times.

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:43.400
It's really important to
recognize that in a table,

00:37:43.400 --> 00:37:46.960
a repeated record
is really just,

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:51.550
in BigQuery, a structure,
an array of structs,

00:37:51.550 --> 00:37:55.450
and you can treat that
as if it were a table.

00:37:55.450 --> 00:37:58.110
There are basically three
kinds of UDFs in BigQuery.

00:37:58.110 --> 00:38:00.884
The obvious one
is the scalar UDF.

00:38:00.884 --> 00:38:02.800
You can pass a string,
and parse something out

00:38:02.800 --> 00:38:03.790
of a string in a UDF.

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:04.810
And you can do
that in JavaScript,

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:06.160
or you can do that in SQL.

00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:07.660
But what's not
necessarily obvious

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:10.390
is that you can aggregate
an array into a structure

00:38:10.390 --> 00:38:14.900
and pass it into a UDF and
treat it like it's a table.

00:38:14.900 --> 00:38:16.510
And really what's
really that then

00:38:16.510 --> 00:38:20.380
allows you to do is fork
queries so that you can act--

00:38:20.380 --> 00:38:23.474
you can take a stream of
data, fork some of the data

00:38:23.474 --> 00:38:25.390
out, and do some really
interesting processing

00:38:25.390 --> 00:38:26.515
and bring back the results.

00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:33.520
So all the aggregate
UDFs follow this pattern.

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:37.120
The array agg struct
construct is really

00:38:37.120 --> 00:38:39.520
building up a sub table.

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:41.260
So that creates a
structure, that creates

00:38:41.260 --> 00:38:42.520
an array of a structure.

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:44.890
And then the UDF, you
pass that through a UDF,

00:38:44.890 --> 00:38:48.670
and the UDF does a
select statement on it

00:38:48.670 --> 00:38:52.030
and then passes the result back.

00:38:52.030 --> 00:38:56.230
Here's an example of a median
function written in SQL.

00:38:56.230 --> 00:38:58.920
You'll notice that the
median function takes it,

00:38:58.920 --> 00:39:04.440
does an array agg on the numbers
that it's going to collect,

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:07.030
and then an unnest
of that array really

00:39:07.030 --> 00:39:09.490
turns that array back into a
table in a select statement.

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:10.406
Does that makes sense?

00:39:13.570 --> 00:39:17.520
And I'm going to do a demo
with some interesting UDFs

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:21.030
that I've built. I'm starting
with a table that really

00:39:21.030 --> 00:39:22.560
consists of just five fields.

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:29.004
It's a name, a gender,
a state, a year,

00:39:29.004 --> 00:39:30.420
and the number of
people that were

00:39:30.420 --> 00:39:35.700
born with that name, that gender
in that state in that year, OK?

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:38.370
So this is from the Social
Security Administration.

00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:41.740
The data goes from 1910 to 1913.

00:39:41.740 --> 00:39:45.250
And it's pretty wild what you
can find with just these five

00:39:45.250 --> 00:39:46.350
fields.

00:39:46.350 --> 00:39:48.240
So can we switch
over to the demo?

00:39:54.420 --> 00:39:59.060
So this is Looker.

00:39:59.060 --> 00:40:01.930
And in the IDE I've
built a little model.

00:40:01.930 --> 00:40:04.590
So for each of the dimensions
that are in the table,

00:40:04.590 --> 00:40:07.600
I've created a LookLM dimension
for the name, state, gender,

00:40:07.600 --> 00:40:10.330
year, and the number was an
integer I wanted as a float,

00:40:10.330 --> 00:40:11.214
so I cast it.

00:40:11.214 --> 00:40:13.630
And I've added a couple of
other measures, a decade, which

00:40:13.630 --> 00:40:16.330
is just divided by
10 for the year.

00:40:16.330 --> 00:40:20.780
I built a sum
aggregate and a count.

00:40:20.780 --> 00:40:22.330
And let's go explore it.

00:40:22.330 --> 00:40:27.160
So this is the Looker interface,
the Looker explorer interface.

00:40:27.160 --> 00:40:28.105
It's pretty simple.

00:40:28.105 --> 00:40:30.070
I can actually--

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:32.699
I can start with the total
number, and the name count,

00:40:32.699 --> 00:40:33.490
and I can run that.

00:40:33.490 --> 00:40:35.980
And I can just see how
much data there is.

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:42.030
So there are 300,000 names.

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:44.610
29,000 different names, right?

00:40:44.610 --> 00:40:48.200
I could add year, and
look at it by year.

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:53.560
Let's sort the year.

00:40:53.560 --> 00:40:56.810
And we go to 1930.

00:40:56.810 --> 00:40:59.160
Let's see.

00:40:59.160 --> 00:41:04.810
So down in-- then
I can see that this

00:41:04.810 --> 00:41:06.490
shows to the most
popular names in 1930.

00:41:06.490 --> 00:41:08.910
So it's just building
a little data model.

00:41:08.910 --> 00:41:12.040
What Looker is doing is that
it's writing queries again

00:41:12.040 --> 00:41:14.320
and feeding them into SQL.

00:41:14.320 --> 00:41:16.030
So everything that we
click on, it really

00:41:16.030 --> 00:41:18.250
ends up being just a SQL query.

00:41:18.250 --> 00:41:19.810
Now above are the
UDFs that I had

00:41:19.810 --> 00:41:21.544
that I built in for this demo.

00:41:21.544 --> 00:41:22.960
But basically,
Looker's just write

00:41:22.960 --> 00:41:24.970
SQL crazy against BigQuery
and returns the results.

00:41:24.970 --> 00:41:26.110
So you get access
to all the data

00:41:26.110 --> 00:41:27.484
no matter what
you're looking at.

00:41:31.450 --> 00:41:35.620
So what we-- let's see.

00:41:38.934 --> 00:41:40.850
So if I want to look at
the most popular name,

00:41:40.850 --> 00:41:42.740
I can look at the name
and then total number,

00:41:42.740 --> 00:41:44.281
and I can find the
most popular name.

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:50.050
It's James, John, Robert,
Michael, William, great.

00:41:50.050 --> 00:41:53.585
That's all terrific

00:41:53.585 --> 00:41:54.335
I can add filters.

00:41:57.460 --> 00:41:59.440
Let's look at female names.

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:06.470
And let's look--
let's do median year.

00:42:11.090 --> 00:42:13.790
So Mary, the median
year for Mary is 1941.

00:42:13.790 --> 00:42:18.860
The median year for
Patricia's 1940, 1951.

00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:20.910
The median year for
Elizabeth is 1966.

00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:23.024
Now in this data, it's
kind of weird, right?

00:42:23.024 --> 00:42:24.440
I just have a state
and the number

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:25.940
of people that were born in
that name with that year.

00:42:25.940 --> 00:42:26.440
So what?

00:42:26.440 --> 00:42:31.012
The median calculation is not
simply just the simple median.

00:42:31.012 --> 00:42:31.970
It's a weighted median.

00:42:31.970 --> 00:42:33.470
I have to take a look at the--

00:42:33.470 --> 00:42:36.080
the median year is what year
were as many people born

00:42:36.080 --> 00:42:38.924
before as after, right?

00:42:38.924 --> 00:42:40.340
And I wanted to
build an aggregate

00:42:40.340 --> 00:42:42.380
for this, which is I wanted
an aggregate function that

00:42:42.380 --> 00:42:42.879
does that.

00:42:42.879 --> 00:42:44.750
So I built a weighted
median function.

00:42:47.716 --> 00:42:49.340
And the weighted
mean, the median year,

00:42:49.340 --> 00:42:51.320
is the median weighted.

00:42:51.320 --> 00:42:53.180
It passes the year and
the number of people

00:42:53.180 --> 00:42:55.040
that were born in that year.

00:42:55.040 --> 00:42:56.990
And I've got a new
aggregate that's in SQL.

00:42:56.990 --> 00:42:58.906
And so if I actually go
look at the code here,

00:42:58.906 --> 00:43:03.660
it really is quite simple.

00:43:03.660 --> 00:43:06.020
I use that weighted
median function

00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:08.330
as if it were a built in
aggregate function, which

00:43:08.330 --> 00:43:09.349
is really kind of cool.

00:43:09.349 --> 00:43:11.390
It's actually taking
something pretty complicated

00:43:11.390 --> 00:43:13.940
and building me this new
concept of weighted median

00:43:13.940 --> 00:43:15.478
that I can use anywhere.

00:43:18.410 --> 00:43:22.470
And let's take a look at how
that function is created.

00:43:22.470 --> 00:43:24.590
So we have-- so I built a--

00:43:24.590 --> 00:43:25.580
this is all open.

00:43:25.580 --> 00:43:27.310
Basically, all of
this is open source

00:43:27.310 --> 00:43:29.310
so you can grab it and
play with it if you want.

00:43:31.290 --> 00:43:32.990
So there's a weighted
median function.

00:43:37.180 --> 00:43:39.250
Here's basically what it is.

00:43:39.250 --> 00:43:40.870
And it basically
takes an array of--

00:43:40.870 --> 00:43:43.261
it takes that array as
input, does this calculation,

00:43:43.261 --> 00:43:44.260
and returns the results.

00:43:44.260 --> 00:43:45.051
Pretty cool, right?

00:43:48.440 --> 00:43:51.080
But you can do also other
kinds of wild things too.

00:43:51.080 --> 00:43:55.350
You can do-- let's
actually filter this.

00:43:55.350 --> 00:43:58.610
So filter this by state.

00:43:58.610 --> 00:44:00.110
And do-- look at Alabama.

00:44:04.006 --> 00:44:04.980
Oh, come on.

00:44:08.890 --> 00:44:12.660
Versus California.

00:44:12.660 --> 00:44:15.500
Let's pivot the state.

00:44:15.500 --> 00:44:21.370
And let's look at
a graph of that.

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:23.760
Now again, all of this is
running as a single SQL query.

00:44:38.030 --> 00:44:42.860
OK, so we can see
that Mary in Alabama,

00:44:42.860 --> 00:44:46.530
the median year for Mary in
Alabama was 1938, not 1951.

00:44:46.530 --> 00:44:48.510
And we can see a little
graph of what it was.

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:50.218
And it is kind of
interesting, because it

00:44:50.218 --> 00:44:55.350
was really popular
in 1931 in Alabama,

00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:57.705
and it peaked in
1984 in California.

00:44:57.705 --> 00:44:59.580
So like maybe it was
Annie Hall or something?

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:01.840
I don't know.

00:45:01.840 --> 00:45:06.370
Like it's pretty wild what
you can see in the data.

00:45:06.370 --> 00:45:08.820
Again, this is just one
table with five columns in it

00:45:08.820 --> 00:45:10.680
and BigQuery on
top and some UDF.

00:45:10.680 --> 00:45:13.242
It's pretty cool, right?

00:45:13.242 --> 00:45:14.590
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:14.590 --> 00:45:15.840
I think it's cool.

00:45:15.840 --> 00:45:18.480
[CHUCKLES]

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:20.435
And if you look
at the SQL for it,

00:45:20.435 --> 00:45:23.390
if we look at the
definition for this,

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:25.030
we can go back and look at the--

00:45:25.030 --> 00:45:26.260
sorry.

00:45:26.260 --> 00:45:29.307
We'll look at back
at the SQL here.

00:45:29.307 --> 00:45:30.890
All the stuff that
is doing the thing,

00:45:30.890 --> 00:45:34.650
but really it's just
a time graph UDF.

00:45:34.650 --> 00:45:36.770
And the time graph
takes the data as input

00:45:36.770 --> 00:45:38.840
and returns an array
of integers, which

00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:40.340
are the amount for the decade.

00:45:40.340 --> 00:45:43.040
And then it converts
that to an image, right?

00:45:43.040 --> 00:45:46.850
So the SQL is just returning
an array of integers, right?

00:45:46.850 --> 00:45:52.100
And so that's pretty
snazzy, right?

00:45:52.100 --> 00:45:55.650
Here's another interesting
function that--

00:45:55.650 --> 00:45:56.806
how am I doing in time?

00:45:56.806 --> 00:45:57.306
Eh.

00:46:05.155 --> 00:46:06.780
Actually, I've got
another demo but I'm

00:46:06.780 --> 00:46:08.700
going to skip that because I
want to keep rolling forward.

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:09.690
Can we go back to the?

00:46:13.767 --> 00:46:15.350
So nesting is the
other thing that you

00:46:15.350 --> 00:46:17.100
can do with BigQuery
that's pretty amazing.

00:46:17.100 --> 00:46:18.560
So the UDFs are really
cool, because you

00:46:18.560 --> 00:46:20.330
can build these aggregates
and do all kinds of wild stuff

00:46:20.330 --> 00:46:20.829
with it.

00:46:20.829 --> 00:46:22.940
But nesting is the
other thing that

00:46:22.940 --> 00:46:25.800
is pretty unusual in
BigQuery in the scale.

00:46:25.800 --> 00:46:31.472
So very often in big databases,
joining two large tables

00:46:31.472 --> 00:46:32.680
can be a huge problem, right?

00:46:36.840 --> 00:46:38.610
Sorry.

00:46:38.610 --> 00:46:41.180
There are things that are
really hard to do when you have

00:46:41.180 --> 00:46:42.540
a couple of large entities.

00:46:42.540 --> 00:46:45.180
So like figuring out
co-occurrence in large tables

00:46:45.180 --> 00:46:48.660
can be very expensive,
navigating graphs of data

00:46:48.660 --> 00:46:50.070
can be very expensive.

00:46:50.070 --> 00:46:53.640
And nesting actually
makes a lot of this stuff

00:46:53.640 --> 00:46:54.850
better and easier.

00:46:54.850 --> 00:46:58.752
So again, if you think
about it, in a nested table,

00:46:58.752 --> 00:47:00.210
basically what you
have is an array

00:47:00.210 --> 00:47:04.830
of structure, a record which is
essentially an array of structs

00:47:04.830 --> 00:47:07.520
that are stored in the table.

00:47:07.520 --> 00:47:09.330
The example, here's
an example of what

00:47:09.330 --> 00:47:10.730
a nested table looks like.

00:47:10.730 --> 00:47:14.710
You have-- this is an order
table with order items.

00:47:14.710 --> 00:47:17.460
An order naturally, if you've
got an e-commerce store that

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:19.770
has orders and you can
order more than one thing,

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:22.060
naturally you'll have an order,
in a traditional SQL database,

00:47:22.060 --> 00:47:24.090
you will have an orders table
and an items table, a line

00:47:24.090 --> 00:47:24.840
items table.

00:47:24.840 --> 00:47:26.490
Or an order and an
order items table.

00:47:26.490 --> 00:47:29.670
But in a nested situation,
what you want is

00:47:29.670 --> 00:47:32.220
you can put those together
because they don't work

00:47:32.220 --> 00:47:33.810
without one another, right?

00:47:33.810 --> 00:47:36.509
An order item really doesn't
exist without an order,

00:47:36.509 --> 00:47:37.800
so that you can place it in it.

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:40.650
And as we can see
here, the structure

00:47:40.650 --> 00:47:43.630
here is an ID, product name,
quantity, and unit price.

00:47:43.630 --> 00:47:45.320
That's an order item structure.

00:47:45.320 --> 00:47:47.329
And then it's repeated
within each order.

00:47:47.329 --> 00:47:48.870
So each of those is
like a little sub

00:47:48.870 --> 00:47:49.828
table within the order.

00:47:52.330 --> 00:47:53.770
Why do you care about this?

00:47:53.770 --> 00:47:59.970
Well, first of all, efficiency.

00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:01.985
And second of all,
computability.

00:48:05.429 --> 00:48:07.720
If you're trying to figure
out what was bought together

00:48:07.720 --> 00:48:11.430
in a really large database,
that's called co-occurrence.

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:16.380
And you'll have a really
hard time figuring out--

00:48:16.380 --> 00:48:18.090
basically, you
can join for free.

00:48:18.090 --> 00:48:20.640
So any time that you
have a nested table,

00:48:20.640 --> 00:48:24.090
it can be up to 100
megabytes in BigQuery.

00:48:24.090 --> 00:48:26.490
You can pass that off.

00:48:26.490 --> 00:48:29.550
You can use a UDF or you can
use another SQL query to process

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:31.440
against it or join against it.

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:37.040
And that happens in a way
that is super efficient.

00:48:37.040 --> 00:48:40.542
So you'll be really surprised
how fast things work.

00:48:40.542 --> 00:48:42.000
Nested data that
you should look at

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:45.811
are things like orders
and order items.

00:48:45.811 --> 00:48:47.310
Sessions and events
are really hard.

00:48:47.310 --> 00:48:50.190
So one of the traditional
things in SQL, in log data,

00:48:50.190 --> 00:48:53.350
is you have somebody--
you sessionize your data.

00:48:53.350 --> 00:48:55.830
You've got your event data,
you roll it up into a session.

00:48:55.830 --> 00:48:57.580
It's a very traditional
MapReduce problem.

00:48:57.580 --> 00:49:00.840
And then you're trying to figure
out who logged in and bought.

00:49:00.840 --> 00:49:03.300
Who signed up today and bought?

00:49:03.300 --> 00:49:05.790
That can be really tricky in
a traditional data warehouse,

00:49:05.790 --> 00:49:08.580
but it's very cheap to
do in a nested situation,

00:49:08.580 --> 00:49:14.580
because you can join the data
twice and find co-occurrence.

00:49:14.580 --> 00:49:16.520
Other places are
user and attributes.

00:49:16.520 --> 00:49:19.181
What you don't want to nest is
something that's not naturally

00:49:19.181 --> 00:49:19.680
part of it.

00:49:19.680 --> 00:49:22.620
So like the user is not really
part of an order or an order

00:49:22.620 --> 00:49:24.900
item, because the
user might appear

00:49:24.900 --> 00:49:26.290
in multiple different orders.

00:49:26.290 --> 00:49:28.235
So you wouldn't nest users.

00:49:28.235 --> 00:49:29.110
Does that make sense?

00:49:35.500 --> 00:49:37.960
When you join nested
table, it works

00:49:37.960 --> 00:49:40.690
just like it does, like you
are joining another table.

00:49:43.270 --> 00:49:45.660
It looks complicated, but
it's actually really simple.

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:47.800
Let's take a look.

00:49:47.800 --> 00:49:49.892
In this case, I've--

